WEBVTT

00:00.180 --> 00:01.650
Instructor: Okay, so now we're gonna talk

00:01.650 --> 00:03.750
about the git commit command,

00:03.750 --> 00:06.420
which is the command we actually use to make a commit

00:06.420 --> 00:09.720
using whatever changes we have previously staged.

00:09.720 --> 00:11.460
But before we actually run the command,

00:11.460 --> 00:12.570
and I definitely recommend

00:12.570 --> 00:14.490
you don't run git commit just yet.

00:14.490 --> 00:15.690
I'll explain why.

00:15.690 --> 00:19.290
Before we run it, we need to discuss commit messages.

00:19.290 --> 00:20.580
Whenever we make a commit,

00:20.580 --> 00:23.100
Git expects us to include a message

00:23.100 --> 00:25.500
that describes or summarizes the changes

00:25.500 --> 00:28.740
included in that snapshot in the given commit.

00:28.740 --> 00:31.680
For example, when I grouped together these files,

00:31.680 --> 00:33.930
the navbar changes, I staged them.

00:33.930 --> 00:36.030
When I committed, in this example,

00:36.030 --> 00:39.720
I added a message, "Add branded navbar."

00:39.720 --> 00:43.170
Or here I change three files, I stage them.

00:43.170 --> 00:44.827
I committed with the message,

00:44.827 --> 00:47.610
"Add team members to about page."

00:47.610 --> 00:50.160
So we'll talk more about writing good commit messages

00:50.160 --> 00:51.930
and what makes a good commit message.

00:51.930 --> 00:53.940
But for now, just know that a commit message

00:53.940 --> 00:57.510
should summarize the changes included in that commit.

00:57.510 --> 01:00.510
So over here on our first novel,

01:00.510 --> 01:02.820
the project that I've been working on,

01:02.820 --> 01:05.280
these two files, these two changes

01:05.280 --> 01:08.040
are really just the creation of those files.

01:08.040 --> 01:10.320
So a commit message might be something like

01:10.320 --> 01:13.440
start novel or initialize characters and outline

01:13.440 --> 01:15.810
or begin work on characters and outline.

01:15.810 --> 01:19.470
Nothing super detailed because it's a very minor commit.

01:19.470 --> 01:20.303
But now let's talk

01:20.303 --> 01:23.370
about actually running the git commit command.

01:23.370 --> 01:25.050
And again, I wanna emphasize

01:25.050 --> 01:27.240
I recommend you don't run it just yet.

01:27.240 --> 01:30.870
This is one option, git commit.

01:30.870 --> 01:32.670
And this will make a commit

01:32.670 --> 01:34.500
with whatever changes have been staged.

01:34.500 --> 01:38.250
However, it's going to try and use your default editor,

01:38.250 --> 01:40.650
whatever your default editor is on your machine,

01:40.650 --> 01:44.280
and most or many students don't have one configured,

01:44.280 --> 01:46.380
which is problematic because it will open up

01:46.380 --> 01:47.820
in something called Vim,

01:47.820 --> 01:50.280
which is a very, very not beginner friendly,

01:50.280 --> 01:53.310
very crazy confusing editor in your terminal.

01:53.310 --> 01:54.810
And if you don't know how to get out of Vim,

01:54.810 --> 01:56.640
it can be very confusing.

01:56.640 --> 01:57.930
If you happen to get in there,

01:57.930 --> 02:00.030
it's :q will get you outta there.

02:00.030 --> 02:02.550
Anyway, I don't recommend you run this yet.

02:02.550 --> 02:05.523
There's another variant, rather than git commit,

02:06.449 --> 02:08.760
which will pop up your editor and ask you for your message,

02:08.760 --> 02:12.480
we can just give git the message at the same time.

02:12.480 --> 02:16.560
So this -m flag, git commit -m,

02:16.560 --> 02:19.410
and then we provide a message in quotes.

02:19.410 --> 02:21.870
Git will use that message that we provide

02:21.870 --> 02:23.340
and make a commit with it,

02:23.340 --> 02:26.970
rather than this flavor which asks us for a message

02:26.970 --> 02:28.710
as a secondary step.

02:28.710 --> 02:29.700
At the end of the day,

02:29.700 --> 02:32.250
there is no difference in the generated commit,

02:32.250 --> 02:33.960
assuming you type the same message.

02:33.960 --> 02:35.010
It's just two different ways

02:35.010 --> 02:37.410
of getting to that same destination.

02:37.410 --> 02:40.080
So I always recommend beginners start with this.

02:40.080 --> 02:41.370
You don't have to configure an editor,

02:41.370 --> 02:44.190
you don't have to worry about Vim or another text editor.

02:44.190 --> 02:47.430
All you do is provide your message and hit enter.

02:47.430 --> 02:50.400
Okay, so -m I believe it stands for message,

02:50.400 --> 02:52.440
or at least that's what I tell myself it stands for.

02:52.440 --> 02:53.820
It makes sense.

02:53.820 --> 02:56.160
Git commit -m and then a message.

02:56.160 --> 02:57.123
So let's try it.

02:58.183 --> 03:00.750
Over here I have my changes are staged.

03:00.750 --> 03:02.820
I'm ready to make my first commit.

03:02.820 --> 03:07.230
So that's exactly what I'm going to do, git commit -m,

03:07.230 --> 03:08.580
and then something simple

03:08.580 --> 03:11.460
that summarizes what I've done in this commit.

03:11.460 --> 03:14.790
How about something like "start work on outline,"

03:14.790 --> 03:19.143
and basic characters or maybe just main characters.

03:21.090 --> 03:24.390
Sure, "Start work on outline and main characters,"

03:24.390 --> 03:25.590
and then I'll hit enter.

03:26.490 --> 03:29.250
Okay, so now what happens here?

03:29.250 --> 03:31.590
Well, let's type git status.

03:31.590 --> 03:33.450
This is what I saw a moment ago.

03:33.450 --> 03:36.570
Changes to be committed, no commits yet.

03:36.570 --> 03:38.430
Now, when I type git status,

03:38.430 --> 03:41.520
nothing to commit, working tree clean.

03:41.520 --> 03:43.620
So this phrase, working tree clean means

03:43.620 --> 03:46.620
that basically everything I've done in my folder

03:46.620 --> 03:49.200
in the working directory Git knows about,

03:49.200 --> 03:52.470
and Git has been tracking and it it's up to date.

03:52.470 --> 03:53.910
Everything is in sync.

03:53.910 --> 03:55.740
But as soon as I change a file,

03:55.740 --> 03:57.750
the working tree is no longer clean,

03:57.750 --> 03:58.770
which is not a bad thing.

03:58.770 --> 04:00.840
But yeah, that's all that it means.

04:00.840 --> 04:04.560
So I've now made my very first commit, first of many.

04:04.560 --> 04:06.390
In the next video, we'll continue

04:06.390 --> 04:08.670
by adding and committing and adding and committing.

04:08.670 --> 04:10.170
We'll get more practice.

04:10.170 --> 04:13.050
I'm gonna stop this video here just to keep it brief.

04:13.050 --> 04:14.580
But just to reiterate,

04:14.580 --> 04:17.430
when we make a commit, we provide a message.

04:17.430 --> 04:20.340
I recommend for now using the -m flag

04:20.340 --> 04:22.590
to provide the message in line.

04:22.590 --> 04:26.130
Otherwise, if you use the regular old git commit command,

04:26.130 --> 04:27.390
you can run into some trouble

04:27.390 --> 04:29.160
if you haven't configured your editor.

04:29.160 --> 04:30.960
And I'll include a video to show you

04:30.960 --> 04:33.300
how to configure that if you're interested.

04:33.300 --> 04:35.550
But it's not something you need to worry about for now.

04:35.550 --> 04:37.710
Whatever message you do provide should summarize

04:37.710 --> 04:39.810
the changes that are included in the commit.

04:39.810 --> 04:41.250
And later on I'll have a video

04:41.250 --> 04:43.980
on best practices for commit messages.

04:43.980 --> 04:45.570
So that's it for this video.

04:45.570 --> 04:48.810
Next up, we'll get more practice doing the same process,

04:48.810 --> 04:51.003
change something, add, commit.
